/* book: lead: An inverter turns DC into AC by switching a load between DC rails. In square-wave operation each switch is on for half the output period, so the load sees a rectangular voltage whose height is fixed by the DC bus and whose frequency is set by the switching rate. The half-bridge uses two switches and a split capacitor bus; the full bridge (H-bridge) uses four switches and gives twice the voltage. E */
