/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Number the switches in the order they are gated: leg A has S1 (top) and S4 (bottom), leg B has S3 and S6, leg C has S5 and S2.
   - In each 60° interval, two terminals share one rail and the third is on the other.
   - The pole voltage vAO, measured from the bus midpoint O, is a ±Vdc/2 square wave.
   - vAB = Σ (4Vdc/nπ) cos(nπ/6) sin n(ωt + π/6) The sum runs over n = 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, …: cos(nπ/6) is zero for n = 3, 9, 15, so no triplen harmonic appears in the line or phase voltage, and the nth harmonic is 1/n of the fundamental.
   - The total rms exceeds the fundamental because of the 5th, 7th, 11th and 13th harmonics.
   - In 120° mode each gate pulse is 120° wide, still one new switch every 60°.
   - With a resistive star load the two connected phases each take Vdc/2 and the floating phase takes zero.
   - The 60° gap between the top and bottom switches of a leg makes accidental shoot-through unlikely, but it gives a lower output from the same bus and poorer switch use.
*/
