/* book Worked example: Q: A three-phase bridge in 180° mode has Vdc = 400 V and feeds a star-connected resistive load of 10 Ω per phase. Find (a) the rms line and phase voltages, (b) the rms phase current and the load power, (c) the rms fundamental phase voltage, and (d) the peak and rms current of each switch.
   1. (a) VL = √(2/3) × 400 = 0.8165 × 400 = 326.6 V. VP = VL/√3 = 326.6/1.732 = 188.6 V.
   2. (b) IP = VP/R = 188.6/10 = 18.86 A. P = 3 IP² R = 3 × 18.86² × 10 = 10667 W.
   3. (c) VP1 = (√2/π) × 400 = 0.4502 × 400 = 180.1 V.
   4. (d) The largest phase current flows in the single phase of each interval: 2Vdc/(3R) = 2 × 400/(3 × 10) = 26.67 A. A top switch carries its phase current for half the cycle, so its rms is IP/√2 = 18.86/1.414 = 13.33 A.
   Answer: VL = 326.6 V, VP = 188.6 V, IP = 18.86 A, P = 10.67 kW, VP1 = 180.1 V; each switch carries 26.67 A peak and 13.33 A rms. */
