/* book Worked example 2: Q: Repeat for 120° mode with the same 400 V bus and 10 Ω star load: find the rms phase voltage and current, the power, the rms line voltage and the peak and rms switch current. Compare with 180° mode.
   1. Each phase is at +200 V for 120°, 0 for 60°, −200 V for 120° and 0 for 60°. VP = √((2 × 120°/360°) × 200²) = 200 × √(2/3) = 163.3 V.
   2. IP = 163.3/10 = 16.33 A. P = 3 × 16.33² × 10 = 8000 W.
   3. Line voltage: two 60° steps at 400 V and four at 200 V in each cycle, so VL = √((2 × 400² + 4 × 200²)/6) = 282.8 V.
   4. Each switch carries Vdc/(2R) = 20 A for 120° of the cycle: rms = 20 × √(1/3) = 11.55 A.
   5. Compared with 180° mode, the power falls from 10667 W to 8000 W (75 %), because each phase is connected for only two thirds of the cycle.
   Answer: VP = 163.3 V, IP = 16.33 A, P = 8000 W, VL = 282.8 V; switch 20 A peak and 11.55 A rms; 120° mode gives 75 % of the 180° power. */
