/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An AC motor needs its voltage to rise with frequency, a UPS must hold its output while the battery runs down, and a heater needs adjustable power.
   - Let the positive half cycle be +Vdc for a pulse of width 2d centred on 90°, and zero for the rest; the negative half cycle mirrors it.
   - Vrms is the rms output in volts and d is half the pulse width in radians (2d/π = 2d°/180°).
   - bn = (4Vdc/nπ) sin(nd), n = 1, 3, 5, … bn is the peak of the nth harmonic in volts.
   - The fundamental follows sin d, not d: near full width a large change of width changes the fundamental very little.
   - Raise the carrier frequency so that it gives p equal pulses per half cycle, each 2d wide and centred at γk = (k − ½)180°/p.
   - With d in degrees the on fraction is 2pd/180.
   - bn = (4Vdc/nπ) sin(nd) Σ sin(nγk) The sum runs over the p pulse centres γk of one half cycle.
   - For the same total on-time the rms is the same as with one pulse, but the spectrum changes: the strongest harmonics move to orders 2p − 1 and 2p + 1, near the carrier, where a smaller filter removes them.
*/
